{-
   `addressOf` over the library's own declaration of it - `lib/Native/Pointer.yana`.

   Written `fn addressOf(it: source'a)` the argument arrives as a loan of the storage it names and
   there is nothing to decide; `lib/` writes `fn addressOf(it: a)`, so a trivially copyable argument
   is *copied* into the call and what reaches the emitter is a scalar in a register.
   `expandAddressOf` is what tells the two apart, and this file was the only place the second
   spelling was compiled while `test/resolve` still had a library of its own.

   The claim is that a `let &` name keeps its identity: `lib/Process/Process.native.yana` writes
   `waitProcess(self.id, addressOf(status), 0)` and reads `status` back afterwards, so an address of
   a *copy* would be a child's exit code written into a temporary nobody looks at. Writing through
   the pointer and reading the name is that program with the syscall taken out, and `addressof
   %status` - taken from the pointer rather than from a copy - is the answer this compiles to.

   A value that is not storage keeps the other meaning, which `test/resolve/Global.yana` records:
   the address of a temporary holding it, which is the only reading available for something that
   cannot be assigned to.
-}

import Native
import Test

@test fn addressOfWritableName():
    let &status = 0 :: Int
    store(addressOf(status), 7)

    checkEqual(status, 7)

@test fn addressOfBorrowedParameter():
    let &value = 3 :: Int
    checkEqual(throughPointer(value), 3)

fn throughPointer(&into: Int) -> Int = *addressOf(into)

@test fn addressOfATemporary():
    -- Not a name, so the storage is a temporary this frame supplies and the pointer names it.
    let p = addressOf(9 :: Int)
    checkEqual(*p, 9)
